WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.692
"What do you think of Tom Sawyer?" asked a reporter from a St. Louis newspaper.

2
00:00:15.693 --> 00:00:19.584
"He's a rascal," said the older woman sharply.

3
00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:24.395
"Finding that gold was just . . . was just so unfortunate."

4
00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:30.378
"Ma'am, don't you mean 'fortunate?'" asked the reporter confused.

5
00:00:30.379 --> 00:00:34.472
"No, I mean unfortunate," said the woman.

6
00:00:34.473 --> 00:00:40.113
"It's unfortunate that such a mischievous boy found the gold."

7
00:00:40.114 --> 00:00:47.882
The reporter had interviewed ten different people about Tom Sawyer, and she had gotten ten different descriptions of the same boy.

8
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:51.795
She truly did not understand what was going on.

9
00:00:51.796 --> 00:00:56.514
What the reporter didn't know was that Tom Sawyer was not a single boy at all.

10
00:00:56.515 --> 00:01:04.338
His character was based on a collection of boys—boys who Samuel Clemens had known when he was growing up in Hannibal, Missouri.

11
00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:12.999
Samuel Clemens is best known as Mark Twain, the author of The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn.

12
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.746
If the reporter had asked Mark Twain about Tom Sawyer, she would have learned that Tom is a smart boy, but not "book smart."

13
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:24.702
She would have learned that Tom is a good boy, but not "church good."

14
00:01:24.703 --> 00:01:30.261
In other words Tom is a completely ordinary child, except for his adventures.

15
00:01:30.262 --> 00:01:33.668
Tom's parents are dead, and Tom is an orphan.

16
00:01:33.669 --> 00:01:37.986
His mother's sister, Polly, has taken him into her house.

17
00:01:37.987 --> 00:01:41.813
Tom's world is the fictional town called Petersburg, Missouri.

18
00:01:41.814 --> 00:01:47.799
Most of Tom's friends lead fairly normal lives, with the exception, of course, of Huckleberry Finn.

19
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.116
Huck, as he is called, is the son of a drunk.

20
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:59.321
Tom, however, sees Huck as a friend who has all the luck—a boy who doesn't have to go to either school or church!

21
00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:03.576
Tom meets a new girl in town named Becky and wins her affection.

22
00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:08.699
She wins Tom's heart too, and some of his adventures happen because of her.

23
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.766
She brings out a sensitive side of mischievous Tom.

24
00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:16.385
Mark Twain's novel is a classic story about a boy.

25
00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:24.299
Tom's youth, independence, and bright curiosity still charm many readers today.
